targets.evidence_quote:
The current study examined the predictive validity of the BCEs scale with respect to three outcomes: caregivers’ psychological distress, sociodemographic risk, and parenting stress. These particular outcomes, which commonly accompany severe poverty and homelessness (
Narayan, 2015
;
Obradović, Shaffer, & Masten, 2012
), are similar to outcomes examined in the original BCEs pilot study, providing replicability (
Narayan et al., 2018
).

targets.outcome_quote:
Consistent with the first hypothesis, means of total BCEs and frequencies of individual items in the current sample were similar to the pilot sample of low-income pregnant women (
Narayan et al., 2018
) for all but one individual item. Notably, homeless parents endorsed the BCEs item about the presence of a predictive home routine significantly less often than low-income pregnant women (68% vs. 81%).
